# {{name}}: the living product repository

What the product must do, why, and how far it is. It is versioned with the code, every milestone reads and
updates it, and `operonx-project brd check` checks it (the test suite runs the check).

| Where | What |
|---|---|
| [`sources/`](sources/README.md) | Every document the requirements come from, registered with its hash. Never edited |
| [`requirements/requirements_traceability.md`](requirements/requirements_traceability.md) | The checklist: each requirement in its original wording, with code, test, evidence and status. This is the only place a requirement's status is stated |
| [`requirements/open_questions.md`](requirements/open_questions.md) | Ambiguities, conflicts, divergences and gaps (`Q-nn`). A question is never deleted, and never resolved without saying so |
| [`board/`](board/README.md) | How the product is built: one file per milestone, with each card's intent, rows, criteria, dependencies, files and status. The engine writes these files |
| [`CHANGELOG.md`](CHANGELOG.md) | One line per change |

Add `features/` (`FEAT-nn`), `decisions/` (`ADR-nnnn`), `ux/screen_inventory.md` (`SCR-…`), `testing/`
(`DEF-nn`) or `assets/` (each file listed in its README) when there is something to put there. The checker
follows these ids when the folders exist.

## Keeping it

- A change meets or alters a requirement: update its row (status, code, test, evidence) and add one line to the
  changelog, then run `operonx-project brd check`.
- A rule changes: never edit a source's text. Record the change in `open_questions.md`, or in an ADR.
- A requirement is removed: keep its row, and say so in its status and note.
- A new version of a source: register it as a new row, and keep the old one.
- A milestone is planned, or a card moves: change nothing by hand. The engine rewrites `board/`, and
  `operonx-project replan` changes a plan.
- State facts only with a source: a source section, a code path, a test or a measurement. Mark assumptions as
  assumptions. A private source is registered by path and hash only, never committed.

## Status

| Status | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `implemented_verified` | Built, and at least one test cited by name exists and passed in the recorded run |
| `implemented_unverified` | Built, with no test that shows it |
| `partially_implemented` | Part of it is built; the row states the gap |
| `planned` | Not built yet; the row names the milestone or card |
| `not_implemented` | Not built, and not planned |
| `blocked` | Cannot be built now; the row says what blocks it and who can unblock it |
| `out_of_scope_demo` | Deliberately left out of this phase |
| `not_applicable` | Does not apply to this product; the row says why |

## The project builder

`operonx-project` (the Project tab in `operonx studio`) builds what the checklist leaves open, one milestone at a
time:

```bash
operonx-project start --title "v1" --goal "..." --rows <ID> <ID>   # a milestone for these rows
operonx-project daemon                                            # plans the cards, builds and checks them
operonx-project status                                            # where every milestone is
```

`.operonx/project/` (outside git) holds only what the running engine needs. When it is missing, it is rebuilt
from `board/`.
